<Wiki
  title="Install"
  description="Install the latest PUV release from github.com/haasele/puv with one command, or build the binary from source."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p>
    One command. It downloads the asset for this machine from the latest GitHub release of
    <a href="https://github.com/haasele/puv">haasele/puv</a> and copies <code>puv</code> to
    <code>~/.local/bin</code>.
  </p>
  <pre class="block">curl -fsSL https://puv.haasele.dev/install.sh | sh</pre>
  <p>
    The script lives at <a href="/install.sh"><code>/install.sh</code></a> on this site. Override
    the destination with <code>PUV_INSTALL_DIR</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>What the script does</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Refuses anything that is not Linux.</li>
    <li>Maps <code>x86_64</code> and <code>aarch64</code> to a glibc target triple. Anything else stops.</li>
    <li>
      Downloads <code>puv-&lt;target&gt;.tar.gz</code> from
      <code>https://github.com/haasele/puv/releases/latest/download/</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      If the same release contains <code>sha256sums.txt</code>, checks the archive. A missing
      checksum file does not fail the install. A present line that does not match does.
    </li>
    <li>Extracts the file named <code>puv</code> and installs it mode <code>0755</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Release files</h2>
  <p>A release the installer can use attaches these names:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>File</th><th>Machine</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>puv-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz</td><td>Linux, glibc, 64-bit Intel/AMD</td></tr>
      <tr><td>puv-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz</td><td>Linux, glibc, 64-bit ARM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>sha256sums.txt</td><td>Optional. Lines are <code>&lt;sha256&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;&lt;filename&gt;</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The tarball may contain <code>puv</code> at the root or one directory down. The script takes
    the first file with that name. “Latest” is whatever GitHub currently marks as the latest
    release, not a version pinned by this website.
  </p>
  <p>
    If that asset is missing, the script exits and prints the source build. It does not fall
    back to a random binary.
  </p>

  <h2>From source</h2>
  <p>Rust stable, edition 2024, is enough. The workspace lockfile is the one to build.</p>
  <pre class="block">git clone https://github.com/haasele/puv
cd puv
cargo install --path crates/puv --locked</pre>
  <p>
    <code>cargo install</code> puts the binary in Cargo’s bin directory, usually
    <code>~/.cargo/bin</code>, not <code>~/.local/bin</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>After install</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv --version
mkdir my-tool && cd my-tool
puv init
puv use 8.4</pre>
  <p>
    <code>puv use</code> is the first network call that fetches PHP. The runtime index is
    StaticPHP’s glibc channel. The next page is
    <a href="/wiki/getting-started">getting started</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Remove</h2>
  <pre class="block">rm "$&#123;PUV_INSTALL_DIR:-$HOME/.local/bin&#125;/puv"</pre>
  <p>
    That deletes the binary. It leaves <code>~/.cache/puv</code> and
    <code>~/.local/share/puv</code>. <code>puv prune --global --all</code> clears the cache
    before you remove the binary. Installed runtimes and global tools stay until
    <code>puv php remove</code> and <code>puv tool uninstall</code>.
  </p>
</Wiki>
